{"number": 11, "text": "                          How      many       letters,       how      many       sounds?\n                          Spelling        and     pronunciation\n\n                         All sections with this symbol (. .) are on the recording. Listen to them while you read this page.\n                         There are 26 letters in the English alphabet.\n                                                                                Z\n                         There are five vowel letters. A E | O U\n                         And there are 21 consonant letters.\n                         But there are more than 40 vowel and consonant sounds in English.\n                         In some words, the number  of letters is the same as the number of sounds.\n                           best       4 letters, 4 sounds          blels|t\n\n                           dentist    7 letters, 7 sounds\n\n\n                         But sometimes the number  of sounds is different from the number of letters.\n                         In green, ee is one sound, and in happy, pp is one sound.\n                     A3a\n                         green        5 letters, 4 sounds          girije                    p | ply\n                                                                            3    4        2    3     4\n                     A3b\n                         In bread, ea is one sound.                           ald\n                         bread        5 letters, 4 sounds                        14\n\n                         In some words  there are silent letters (letters with no sound). In listen, t is silent.\n                         listen       6 letters, 5 sounds.\n\n\n                         In some words, one letter is two sounds. The x in six is two sounds like k + s.\n                     A3d\n                         six          3 letters, 4 sounds          slilx\n                                                                       2|3|4\n\n                         We  sometimes write the same sound differently in different words. For example, the e in red\n                         sounds like the ea in bread.\n                         Sometimes  two words have the same pronunciation but different spellings. (See Section E8\n                     Ada\n                         Homophones.)\n\n\n                         know  — no\n                         A: Do you  know?  B: No, | don’t.\n                     A4b\n                         And  sometimes two words  have the same spelling but\n\n                         different pronunciations.\n                         read (infinitive and present tense) — read (past tense)\n                         A: Do you  want to read the newspaper?\n                            No         I vead i+                                             <4\n                     A4c\n                         Because there are more sounds than letters, we use symbols for pronunciation.\n                         /best/ best                                                          coffee   /'Itsan/ listen\n                         /Ori:/ three /sitks/six                /bred/ bread   /nau/no                        red\n                         /red/ read (past tense)      read (infinitive and present tense)\n                         The symbol  ' (look at the beginning of the symbols for dentist, happy, coffee, listen) comes\n                         before stressed syllables (see Section B Syllables and words).\n\n                            English Pronunciation in Use (Elementary)", "char_count": 3517, "mean_conf": 84.28, "word_count": 382, "words": [{"text": "A3a", "x0": 384, "y0": 1333, "x1": 435, "y1": 1357, "conf": 92, "bold": false}, {"text": "A3b", "x0": 384, "y0": 1519, "x1": 436, "y1": 1555, "conf": 80, "bold": false}, {"text": "A3d", "x0": 383, "y0": 1916, "x1": 435, "y1": 1941, "conf": 93, "bold": false}, {"text": "Ada", "x0": 382, "y0": 2253, "x1": 434, "y1": 2277